From 9accb1f9f4a2aebea2bbf7d8748a34ba33b0cdde Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: forge-shepherd <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 00:40:46 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(hooks): flag-agnostic enforcement predicate +
 config-driven protected paths (refs eda6d866)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Per review: gate each hook on a SINGLE resolved predicate so whichever flag we
land on for the TDD off-switch drops in with no rework, and make protected-path
config-driven end to end.

- forge-native-hook.js: add isEnforcementActive('tdd'|'protected-path') — the one
  place each enforcement kind is resolved. protected-path now builds its matcher
  from the resolved `protectedPaths` list (config is the source of truth): unset →
  built-in fallback set (fail toward enforcement / back-compat), [] → inert, list →
  protect exactly those paths/globs (globToRegExp: ** spans separators, * within a
  segment). Shell-command scan honors the same resolved matcher.
- setup.js: describeHookEnforcement() prints the resolved TDD/protected-path state
  after installing hooks and the header no longer over-claims "TDD enforcement" —
  a disabled gate installs an inert hook and setup now says so.
- Tests: predicate + glob + config-driven matching + honest-summary coverage.

HELD (awaiting product decision): the TDD off-switch mechanism itself (unlock the
L1 rail.tdd_intent vs a separate toggle). isEnforcementActive('tdd') already reads
whichever flag lands, so no rework needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
